## 4. Scope

### 4.1 In scope

The first corpus SHALL cover:

- TypeScript and JavaScript source included by supported project configurations;
- TypeScript-bearing framework artifacts, initially including `.svelte` files participating in the supported SvelteKit check/build pipeline;
- TypeScript ASTs;
- declarations, symbol tables, references, scopes, and name resolution;
- static types, type relationships, and the type graph;
- package, module, import, and dependency graphs and their distinct edge taxonomies;
- call sites and call graphs;
- control-flow graphs;
- data-flow and taint-flow graphs;
- a code property graph or a precisely bounded equivalent composition of semantic graphs;
- tests and test-to-code relationships;
- test coverage observations;
- runtime traces and trace-to-code relationships;
- repository snapshots and working-tree change sets;
- architecture rules and dependency constraints;
- analyzer runs, semantic facts, queries, Analyzer Finding Records, Engineering Evidence Records, uncertainty, and exceptions;
- change-impact analysis and before/after semantic comparison;
- coding-agent queries, planning evidence, implementation gates, and completion evidence;
- later qualification and integration of analysis providers under W0-15; Stage A selects none.

### 4.2 Initial repository subject

The first concrete subject is:

```text
JanumiCode/janumiprofessionalworkbench
```

The root manifest, not filesystem proximity, defines the first workspace perimeter.

**Semantically supported project set**

- root discovery and configuration context: `package.json`, `bun.lock`, `bunfig.toml`, `turbo.json`, root `tsconfig.json`, `.gitignore`, ESLint configuration, Prettier configuration and exclusions, dependency-cruiser configuration, and Sonar configuration;
- shared compiler configuration under `packages/typescript-config`;
- source and build project configurations for:
  - `packages/rph-application`;
  - `packages/rph-assurance`;
  - `packages/rph-authoring`;
  - `packages/rph-contracts`;
  - `packages/rph-domain`;
  - `packages/rph-engine`;
  - `packages/rph-persistence`;
  - `packages/rph-ports`;
  - `packages/rph-product-realization-pwa`;
  - `packages/rph-projections`;
- authored TypeScript, JavaScript, and `.svelte` source in `apps/rph-demo` that participates in its configured SvelteKit check/build pipeline, subject to the generated-context qualification below;
- included package/app unit tests;
- generator source and generated TypeScript as distinct artifact classes;
- workspace manifests, package exports, lockfile-resolved dependencies, and existing architecture rules as contextual graph inputs.

Normal and `tsconfig.build.json` project variants remain distinct because their inclusion boundaries differ.

`apps/rph-demo/tsconfig.json` extends the generated `.svelte-kit/tsconfig.json`. The present generated configuration identifies the app include surface, but its freshness was not established because the write-producing `svelte-kit sync` operation was not authorized. Compiler-confirmed app support therefore SHALL retain the generated configuration's revision-bound provenance, digest, and freshness basis; until that evidence exists, the app project context SHALL be reported as configured-but-unverified or partial rather than compiler-confirmed.

**Inventory-only perimeter**

The corpus inventories, but does not initially claim compiler-complete semantic support for:

- deterministic Playwright tests, configuration, setup, and teardown outside the SvelteKit project include set;
- live/network Playwright surfaces;
- executable static JavaScript not included by a declared project context;
- framework-generated `.svelte-kit` artifacts other than the consumed generated `tsconfig.json` configuration context, which remains explicit and freshness-qualified;
- any current gate exclusion or executable seam not covered by a declared project configuration.

Each perimeter region SHALL remain labeled supported, partial, unsupported, or not analyzed. No perimeter region SHALL disappear into an implicit empty result.

**First-subject exclusions**

The supported semantic subject excludes:

- `node_modules`, `dist`, `build`, `.turbo`, coverage output, Playwright result output, caches, and temporary databases;
- TypeScript projects and code prototypes under `docs/**`, except CSAA documents as authoring artifacts;
- `apps/rph-demo/harness/**` workflow-runtime JavaScript;
- third-party source beyond identity, manifest, lockfile, advisory, and provenance metadata;
- live agent/network execution and production trace collection;
- sibling repositories under `E:/Projects/hestami-ai`.

**Revision and execution mode**

Stage A does not freeze a preparation-time commit as timeless architecture. Every inventory SHALL preserve the received working tree, SHALL bind facts to the parent commit plus explicit worktree/change-set identity and relevant content/configuration digests, SHALL record an observation time, and SHALL reverify at review. A parent commit alone never identifies a dirty subject.

Accordingly, a parent commit alone SHALL NOT be treated as sufficient identity for a dirty subject.

Inspection is read-only by default. Installation, formatting, generation, builds, live/network tests, external scanners, production traces, and any existing command that writes caches or derived artifacts SHALL require separate execution authorization. A configured but unexecuted command SHALL be recorded as unexecuted and SHALL NOT be inferred to pass.

The checked-in execution and tooling surfaces currently include Bun, Turborepo, TypeScript, ESLint, Prettier, dependency-cruiser, Vitest, Vite/SvelteKit and `svelte-check`, Playwright configuration, and Sonar configuration. Those are repository observations, not provider selections or approvals. `JAN-CSAA-005` SHALL verify their exact roles against its analyzed revision and SHALL NOT copy them forward as timeless architecture.

Generated or virtual TypeScript used to analyze a framework artifact SHALL retain provenance and location mappings to the originating authored artifact and SHALL NOT be reported as authored source.

### 4.3 Explicitly out of scope

The first corpus SHALL NOT define:

- Kubernetes, Pulumi, Helm, network-policy, RBAC, cloud, or other infrastructure-graph assurance;
- PWA, PWU, Undertaking, Professional Work Graph, or professional-cognition semantics;
- a universal assurance engine spanning code, infrastructure, and professional work;
- intent-to-implementation or professional-objective traceability;
- Janumi Platform deployment topology;
- a new Janumi product, PWA, canonical ontology, or canonical role;
- embeddings as authoritative semantic facts;
- automatic governance Decisions, waivers, approvals, or Baseline promotion;
- source-code rewriting or autonomous remediation as part of the analysis core;
- language-general semantics beyond what is required to bound TypeScript interoperation.

CSAA MAY consume an externally governed software contract, architecture rule, or invariant as an opaque, identified constraint. It SHALL NOT model, interpret, or assert realization of PWA, PWU, Undertaking, professional objective, or any other professional-semantic object.

Code-to-professional-object traceability SHALL require a separately governed adapter and corpus.

Future integration MAY link code evidence to Janumi professional objects, but that is a separate governed boundary. It SHALL NOT be smuggled into the TypeScript semantic model.

### 4.4 Scope extension rule

A proposed extension to another language, infrastructure representation, or professional-work domain SHALL identify:

1. the new source of factual truth;
2. the new semantic model;
3. the new completeness and uncertainty limits;
4. the new assurance questions;
5. the adapters that remain reusable;
6. the concepts that must remain domain-specific.

Shared storage or query machinery alone SHALL NOT justify collapsing distinct semantic universes.

---

## 5. Normative language for the proposed corpus

In each future authorized document `JAN-CSAA-001` through `JAN-CSAA-011`:

- **SHALL** and **SHALL NOT** state mandatory requirements;
- **SHOULD** and **SHOULD NOT** state strong defaults that require recorded justification when not followed;
- **MAY** states permission;
- examples are informative and SHALL NOT narrow a requirement;
- each mandatory requirement SHALL have a stable requirement ID;
- requirement IDs SHALL NOT be reused after publication;
- each mandatory requirement SHALL identify its verification method or an explicit reason verification is deferred.

A sentence lacking a normative keyword or an explicitly ledgered equivalent gate SHALL NOT be treated as a mandatory requirement; it is present-state description, rationale, or other informative context.

While this document is Draft or Proposed, its uppercase clauses are candidate requirements. The W0-01 through W0-16 decisions govern only through `REG-D-017`; their appearance here is administrative carriage, not a second source of authority. Every other clause becomes binding only within the status and authority later conferred on the applicable artifact.

This pre-adoption candidate uses section-scoped SHALL language to state candidate commissioning and closure criteria. Before this document may move to Proposed, every retained mandatory clause SHALL receive a stable requirement ID and verification binding in the charter ledger, and the self-review SHALL close. No implementation SHALL rely on this pre-adoption candidate as authority.

---

## 6. Source-of-truth and authority model

The phrase “the implementation is the source of truth” SHALL NOT be used unless its concern is explicit.

The corpus SHALL use the distinctions below.

### 6.1 Five distinct truth and authority questions

| Question | Source to inspect | What it may establish | What it cannot establish alone |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| What code exists at this exact subject revision? | Repository content, configuration, generated files, and content digests | Observed implementation fact | Intended behavior, acceptance, or professional authority |
| What exact governed shape is expected? | Enforced schemas, generated contracts, migrations, and conformance fixtures | Executable reference shape | Meaning outside the shape's governed scope |
| What is the implementation intended to mean or preserve? | Concern-owning canon, ratified specification, registered working authority, or an ADR incorporated through one of those recognized authority paths | Intended semantics, constraints, and architectural obligations | Whether the current code actually conforms |
| What did a particular execution do? | Version-bound runtime traces, logs, events, coverage, and test results | Observed behavior for the captured executions | Behavior for unobserved paths or all possible inputs |
| What does an analyzer conclude? | Revision-bound analysis run with provenance and coverage declaration | Derived evidence, Analyzer Finding Record, or bounded inference | Independent authority, complete truth, approval, or absence of risk |

### 6.2 Controlling source rule

For **facts about the existing implementation**, repository content SHALL be the primary observed source.

For **intended product or software meaning during the current convergence phase**, the concern-owning canon, governed specification, registered working authority, or an ADR incorporated through one of those recognized authority paths SHALL control. An independently “accepted” ADR SHALL NOT have authority under this corpus.

For **exact governed boundary shapes**, enforced repository reference artifacts SHALL control.

For **a concrete execution**, captured runtime evidence SHALL control only for that execution and environment.

Analyzer output SHALL remain derived evidence about an identified subject. It SHALL NOT become a second source tree, silently override an enforced reference artifact, or confer governance authority.

### 6.3 Revision-bound evidence

Every semantic fact, graph element, coverage observation, trace link, query result, and Analyzer Finding Record SHALL be bound to enough subject identity to determine what was analyzed.

For a committed clean tree, subject identity SHOULD include:

- repository identity;
- commit identifier;
- relevant submodule or workspace identity;
- TypeScript project/configuration identity;
- configuration and lockfile digests;
- analyzer and rule-set versions.

For a dirty or synthetic working tree, a commit identifier alone is insufficient. Subject identity SHALL additionally include content digests or a reproducible change-set identity covering every analyzed input.

### 6.4 Freshness and invalidation

Evidence SHALL be marked stale or invalidated when a change can affect its conclusion, including changes to:

- source or generated code;
- TypeScript configuration;
- package resolution or lockfiles;
- analyzer configuration or rules;
- analyzer versions;
- coverage instrumentation;
- test selection;
- runtime build or deployment identity;
- framework models or semantic adapters.

The absence of an invalidation edge SHALL NOT be interpreted as freshness.

### 6.5 Completeness and uncertainty

The system SHALL distinguish at least:

- confirmed extracted fact;
- bounded inference;
- unsupported construct;
- incomplete analysis;
- conflicting evidence;
- stale evidence;
- not analyzed;
- no Analyzer Finding Record under the declared analysis coverage.

Exact serialized spellings belong to `JAN-CSAA-007`.

“No Analyzer Finding Record” SHALL NOT be rendered or reported as “no defect” unless the applicable Analysis Rule Profile or governing Assurance Policy defines a complete decision procedure for the exact subject and coverage.

### 6.6 Ratified Stage A semantic baselines

The program SHALL preserve the following effective Stage A semantic baselines exactly as constraints from `REG-D-017`. Their exact technical performance remains allocated to later governed members and enforced artifacts, which SHALL NOT weaken them.

| Decision | Ratified semantic baseline | Deferred ground |
| --- | --- | --- |
| W0-09 | A TypeScript or TypeScript-bearing Svelte fact is compiler-confirmed only for the exact source, project configuration, compiler/toolchain version, module-resolution environment, and relevant generated context against which it was derived. Analyzer and adapter output remains derived evidence, never independent semantic authority. | Analyzer-provider selection |
| W0-10 | Coverage is version-bound evidence that identified code regions were exercised by identified executions under identified instrumentation. A percentage alone is neither a correctness oracle nor evidence about unexecuted behavior. | Provider, metric family, aggregation, exclusions, and thresholds |
| W0-11 | A runtime trace supports claims only about its identified captured execution, environment, inputs, instrumentation, and observation limits. It does not establish behavior for unobserved paths or all possible inputs. | Production collection, ingestion, retention, privacy, and sampling |
| W0-12 | Analyzer observations, findings, dispositions, suppressions, and corrections preserve append-only logical history. Corrections cite and supersede prior records; physical compaction must preserve lossless reconstruction. | Physical persistence and retention design |
| W0-13 | Source rewriting, repair approval, repair application, and automated remediation remain outside the analysis core. Proposed remediation requires a separately authorized change and revalidation workflow. | Concrete remediation orchestration |
| W0-14 | Unsupported, failed, stale, partially analyzed, excluded, or incomplete regions remain explicit and may not collapse into an empty finding set, passing verdict, or unqualified green result. | Final aggregate and repository-gate presentation rules |

---

## 7. Core non-equivalences

The corpus SHALL preserve these distinctions:

```text
Source code                  ≠ AST
AST                          ≠ symbol or type semantics
Import graph                 ≠ package-manifest dependency graph
Package-manifest graph       ≠ lockfile-resolved dependency graph
Lockfile-resolved graph      ≠ inferred or observed runtime dependency graph
Call graph                   ≠ all possible dynamic dispatch
CFG                          ≠ runtime trace
DFG                          ≠ complete information flow
CPG                          ≠ repository knowledge graph
Semantic index               ≠ source of authority
Analyzer                     ≠ canonical Assurance Policy or Analysis Rule Profile
Analyzer execution           ≠ assurance satisfaction
Analyzer Finding Record      ≠ governance Decision
Analyzer-record suppression  ≠ defect removal
Tool agreement               ≠ correctness
Test pass                    ≠ behavioral preservation
Coverage percentage          ≠ correctness
Observed runtime path        ≠ all reachable behavior
Repository commit            ≠ Janumi Baseline
Absence of evidence          ≠ evidence of absence
```

### 7.1 Canonical assurance boundary and technical analysis records

Canonical **Assurance Policy**, **Assurance Observation**, Assessment, Evidence, and governance meanings remain owned by `JPWB-DOC-002` and `JPWB-DOC-003`. This corpus SHALL reference those meanings where integration is required and SHALL NOT create a parallel canonical `Finding` entity.

Within the technical code-analysis layer, the corpus SHALL use the following definitions:

- an **Analysis Capability** defines a kind of fact or Engineering Evidence Record that can be produced;
- an **Analyzer Provider** is a concrete tool or adapter that implements one or more capabilities;
- an **Analysis Rule Profile** is a versioned set of executable or declarative checks for a defined repository scope;
- an **Analyzer Finding Record** preserves a provider observation or bounded conclusion with subject, provenance, limitations, and disposition history;
- an **Engineering Evidence Record** is a non-canonical technical record that binds an observed or derived result to its subject, producer, method, coverage, time, and epistemic limits;
- a **Repository Gate Profile** is a versioned technical definition that selects Analysis Rule Profiles and declares applicability, evidence, criteria, epistemic outcomes, severity, and blocking behavior;
- a **Repository Gate Evaluation** is a revision-bound execution result produced by applying one identified Repository Gate Profile; it SHALL preserve rather than redefine the profile and SHALL NOT erase its inputs or underlying Analyzer Finding Records;
- an **Engineering Exception Record** is an explicit, scoped, approved disposition under operative engineering-practice and concern-owning rules; a configuration suppression without that record is not an exception.

These technical records MAY supply Evidence or inputs to canonical assurance only through an explicitly governed mapping. Analyzer execution or a Repository Gate Evaluation SHALL NOT by itself create an Assurance Assessment, resolve an Assurance Observation, exercise governance, or promote a Baseline.

### 7.2 CPG and repository semantic graph

A code property graph MAY be the storage representation for AST, control-flow, data-flow, and call relationships. The corpus SHALL NOT assume that this automatically makes it the complete repository semantic graph.

Coverage, runtime traces, analyzer runs, Analyzer Finding Records, Engineering Evidence Records, project configurations, snapshots, architecture rules, and tool provenance SHALL receive explicit models even when stored in the same graph database.

---

## 8. Proposed corpus progression

```text
JAN-CSAA-000
Corpus scope, authority boundaries, vocabulary, and manifest
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-001
Capability architecture and responsibility boundaries
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-002
Program TypeScript semantic model and invariants
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-003
Analysis, enrichment, query, and change-impact semantics
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-004
Analysis-rule, analyzer-finding, provider, and repository-gate catalog
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-006
Independent golden repository and change-scenario fixture
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-007
Versioned machine contracts and adapter interfaces
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-008
Executable analyzer conformance and V&V specification
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-009
Indexing, persistence, incremental update, recovery, and operations
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-010
Coding-agent analysis employment profile
    ↓
JAN-CSAA-011
Qualified TypeScript and tool-integration standard
    ↓
Repository-specific implementation roadmap and requirement ledger
```

`JAN-CSAA-005` is a parallel current-state evidence feed into `JAN-CSAA-001` through `JAN-CSAA-004`, the fixture, and implementation planning. It does not sit downstream as semantic authority: it maps observed code and configuration to concern-owning obligations.

The vertical path is an informative semantic-dependency illustration, not a drafting or commissioning chronology. Concrete-tool conformance obligations are owned by §§10.4 and 10.7–10.8.

### 8.1 Structural role mapping to the RPH exemplar

This is a structural analogy only; it does not import RPH professional-work semantics or authority.

| RPH corpus function | CSAA allocation | Code-analysis adaptation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 000 — product boundary, vocabulary, and manifest | 000 | Scope, authority boundary, technical vocabulary, manifest, and commissions |
| 001 — capability/product architecture | 001 | Tool-neutral code-analysis capability architecture |
| 002 — semantic object model and invariants | 002 | TypeScript/repository semantic model and cross-graph invariants |
| 003 — specialization semantics | 003 | Extraction, enrichment, query, inference, and change-impact semantics |
| 004 — assurance catalog | 004 | Technical Analysis Rule Profiles, Repository Gate Profiles/Evaluations, and analyzer-provider contract |
| 005 — current/legacy semantic inventory | 005 | Revision-bound JPWB repository inventory and conformance mapping |
| 006 — independently reviewable reference fixture | 006 | Golden repository and semantic change scenarios |
| 007 — machine contracts | 007 | Snapshot, graph, query, analysis-record, and adapter contracts |
| 008 — executable conformance | 008 | Analyzer, invariant, incremental, security, and trajectory V&V |
| 009 — persistence and migration | 009 | Index persistence, invalidation, recovery, isolation, and operations |
| 010 — user-facing employment | 001, 003, 007, and 010 by single-owner concern | Human inspection boundaries plus coding-agent analysis employment |
| CSAA-specific provider realization | 011 | Qualification and binding of concrete TypeScript and analysis tools |

