| `JAN-CSAA-011` | `JAN-CSAA-011 - TypeScript and Analysis Tool Integration Standard.md` | Unaudited and unauthored | Reserved; later wave not commissioned | None | Provider roles, qualification, licensing, sandboxing, and integration |

### 9.1 Identifier and version rules

`REG-D-017` confirms the family prefix and reservations. For every authored controlled document:

- its permanent document ID SHALL remain unchanged across title, filename, location, version, and status changes;
- its permanent document ID SHALL NOT be reassigned;
- versions SHALL use `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`;
- each artifact SHALL carry status, settledness, and authority as separate fields;
- **Draft** status SHALL mean in authoring with no program authority;
- **Proposed** status SHALL mean ledger-closed and self-reviewed, ready for independent review/adoption, with no program authority;
- **Normative** status SHALL mean an active HYPOTHESIS-grade program working reference only after its own exact-version/digest `JPWB-REG-005` sponsor conferral;
- **Deprecated** status SHALL mean discoverable and temporarily usable only under its stated compatibility rule;
- **Superseded** status SHALL mean no longer current and identify its successor;
- status and version SHALL remain independent;
- manifest metadata and document metadata SHALL change together.

### 9.2 Filename rule

After identifier confirmation, new corpus files SHALL use:

```text
<PERMANENT-ID> - <Canonical Title>.md
```

`JAN-CSAA-000` is the explicit manifest exception and remains `README.md`. The convention makes every other controlled identity visible without depending on Git rename detection or title stability.

### 9.3 Required controlled-document metadata

Every authored controlled `JAN-CSAA` document, including this charter, SHALL declare:

- permanent document ID, canonical title, version, controlled status, and settledness;
- authority status and the exact registered sponsor grant or ratification record;
- parent and inherited authorities;
- `Governs` and `Does Not Govern` statements;
- subject scope and applicability;
- precedence and conflict-routing rules;
- normative-language convention;
- requirement-ledger location and verification owner;
- change authority and change procedure;
- supersedes and superseded-by relationships, when any;
- companion enforced artifacts and conformance-test references.

`JAN-CSAA-005` SHALL additionally declare the analyzed repository revision and worktree state, as-of date, evidence provenance, last verification time, and the condition under which the inventory becomes stale or is superseded.

### 9.4 Per-member adoption, review, and oracle controls

Until a later registered delegation establishes another model:

- the accountable sponsor retains authority to confer Normative program status on each CSAA member;
- every conferral SHALL be a distinct `JPWB-REG-005` sponsor `DECISION`;
- an author MAY move a member from Draft to Proposed only after closing its requirement ledger and self-review;
- an author, reviewer, analyzer, or program-local log SHALL NOT confer Normative status;
- every Proposed member SHALL receive independent adversarial review;
- every adoption package SHALL identify the exact document ID, semantic version, SHA-256 digest of the candidate bytes, review identity and method, requirement ledger, verification evidence, unresolved findings, strongest opposing case, consequences of adoption, amendment, rejection, and deferral, and recommendation;
- every unresolved fork and every material normative change SHALL receive its own full-judgment decision surface and individual sponsor response field;
- the candidate digest SHALL be rechecked immediately before sponsor recording;
- one controlled changeset SHALL append the conferral to `JPWB-REG-005` and synchronize member and manifest status, version, and authority fields;
- a candidate-byte change after sponsor disposition SHALL pause conferral for affected review unless the change is an exactly previewed administrative carriage;
- pre-existing oracle judgments, acceptance criteria, fixtures, gate configurations, and other judgment-grain reference artifacts SHALL change only through separately authorized review;
- newly authored oracle content SHALL remain Proposed until conferred;
- a suspected-wrong oracle SHALL create a divergence record rather than an inline weakening;
- the Wave 0 ledger and review templates SHALL be used as mandatory starting points, with all required fields preserved.

A synchronized adoption record uses `EFFECTIVE — MERGED`. If synchronized carriage cannot be completed in that controlled changeset, the record SHALL say `EFFECTIVE — MERGE PENDING`; a new append-only entry SHALL later confirm carriage, and the original entry SHALL NOT be rewritten.

---

## 10. Reserved responsibilities and staged document commissions

The sections below reserve semantic ownership and describe candidate deliverables. They do not authorize file creation by themselves.

- W0-08 approves `JAN-CSAA-001`, `JAN-CSAA-002`, and `JAN-CSAA-005` in principle for Wave 1, but that commission is inactive until W0-17.
- `JAN-CSAA-003`, `JAN-CSAA-004`, and `JAN-CSAA-006` through `JAN-CSAA-011` are reserved for later waves and are not commissioned.
- No document in this section is authored, adopted, or authoritative merely because its responsibility is described here.

### 10.1 `JAN-CSAA-001` — Capability architecture

This document SHALL define the tool-neutral system context and responsibility boundaries.

It SHALL cover:

- repository acquisition and subject identification;
- workspace, package, and TypeScript project discovery;
- source/configuration classification;
- semantic extraction;
- graph normalization and composition;
- test and coverage ingestion;
- runtime-trace ingestion;
- analysis scheduling and orchestration;
- Engineering Evidence Record and Analyzer Finding Record normalization;
- query and change-impact services;
- coding-agent and human interfaces;
- provider/plugin boundaries;
- sandbox, filesystem, process, and network trust boundaries;
- secrets, source confidentiality, and retention;
- degraded operation, partial analysis, timeout, cancellation, and recovery;
- observability for every material analysis and tool invocation;
- scalability and responsiveness objectives without choosing a storage engine prematurely.

The architecture SHALL distinguish logical responsibilities from deployable services. It SHALL NOT require a microservice, graph database, daemon, or language-server topology merely because a diagram contains separate boxes.

Acceptance of `JAN-CSAA-001` SHALL require:

- an end-to-end data-flow diagram;
- a responsibility matrix;
- a trust-boundary and threat model;
- defined failure and degraded modes;
- explicit non-goals;
- at least two credible architecture alternatives for unresolved storage and orchestration choices.

### 10.2 `JAN-CSAA-002` — TypeScript semantic model and invariants

This document SHALL define the stable semantic objects and relationships independent of provider-specific output.

The object catalog SHALL address at least:

- Repository;
- Repository Revision and parentage;
- Repository Snapshot;
- VCS commit, ref or branch, merge base, candidate merge, and worktree identity;
- Working Change Set;
- Workspace;
- Package;
- package manifest, lockfile, external component identity, resolved dependency version, advisory, and vulnerability observation;
- TypeScript Project and configuration;
- authored Source Artifact, including TypeScript-bearing framework artifacts;
- Generated or Virtual Artifact and generator/source-origin relation;
- governed software Contract, schema, API boundary, compatibility rule, and generated-contract artifact;
- build configuration, build execution, emitted or bundled Build Artifact, and execution-artifact identity;
- Source Map and generated-to-authored source-location mapping;
- Module;
- declaration;
- symbol;
- scope;
- reference;
- type;
- type relation;
- generic or type parameter and constraint;
- overload set, overload signature, and implementation signature;
- namespace and module augmentation;
- decorator or annotation occurrence;
- class, interface, function, method, parameter, variable, property, and signature;
- expression, statement, and control-flow node;
- import/export relation;
- workspace/package-manifest dependency;
- lockfile-resolved external dependency and version;
- inferred runtime dependency and observed runtime load;
- call site and callable target;
- data-flow source, transformation, sanitizer, and sink;
- instrumentation configuration and instrumentation site;
- test suite, test, test case, test target, test selection, test run, test result, and assertion outcome;
- coverage observation, coverage region, denominator basis, and measurement granularity;
- runtime build/environment identity, workload selection, execution, Trace Schema, Trace Collector, trace, span, and event;
- runtime-artifact and runtime-to-authored-source relation;
- repository history and change relation;
- architecture rule;
- analysis run;
- semantic fact;
- query;
- Analyzer Finding Record;
- Engineering Evidence Record;
- exception or suppression reference.

For each object and relationship, the document SHALL define:

- identity;
- subject revision;
- provenance;
- ownership;
- lifecycle;
- authority and epistemic limits;
- required and optional metadata;
- versioning and invalidation;
- supported, inferred, unknown, and conflicting states;
- serialization owner;
- cross-graph invariants.

Required invariants SHALL include:

- no semantic fact without subject and provenance;
- no cross-revision edge presented as same-revision fact;
- no provider-normalized fact that loses its raw provider trace;
- no coverage observation detached from execution artifact, instrumentation configuration, denominator basis, measurement granularity, and test or workload selection;
- no runtime observation detached from execution artifact, trace schema and collector configuration, environment, workload selection, and time boundary;
- no coverage or trace-to-source relation without a source-origin mapping where transformation occurred;
- no inferred edge presented as compiler-confirmed;
- no graph completeness claim without a declared coverage basis;
- no Analyzer Finding Record state change that erases prior Engineering Evidence Records.

Program-dependence graph and static-single-assignment representations MAY be defined as derived optional views when a qualified analysis capability needs them. They SHALL NOT be silently treated as core facts, provider-independent ground truth, or substitutes for their source CFG and DFG provenance.

### 10.3 `JAN-CSAA-003` — Analysis, enrichment, query, and change impact

This document SHALL define the meaning and limits of:

- parsing and AST extraction;
- symbol and reference resolution;
- type analysis;
- dependency analysis;
- call-graph construction;
- control-flow construction;
- data-flow and taint analysis;
- CPG construction or graph composition;
- project references, path aliases, conditional exports, declaration files, source maps, module augmentation, decorators, JSX/TSX, and framework-generated TypeScript;
- test discovery and test-to-code mapping;
- coverage ingestion and comparison;
- runtime-trace correlation;
- generated-code and declaration-file handling;
- framework and reflection modeling;
- architecture discovery;
- semantic enrichment and bounded inference;
- semantic queries;
- code slicing;
- change-impact analysis;
- before/after graph comparison.

Every capability SHALL state:

- supported subjects and constructs;
- required inputs;
- produced fact and edge types;
- soundness or completeness goal;
- known false-positive and false-negative classes;
- unsupported behavior;
- inference and confidence treatment;
- incremental invalidation boundary;
- required provenance;
- expected failure behavior;
- declared capability-conformance oracle.

The document SHALL prohibit a query from silently treating unknown, unsupported, stale, or incomplete regions as empty.

### 10.4 `JAN-CSAA-004` — Analysis-rule, repository-gate-profile, and analyzer-provider contract

This document SHALL allocate at least one appropriate technical Analysis Rule Profile to each assurance area below and SHALL define Repository Gate Profiles only for areas separately designated as binding gates. This charter does not designate every area as a repository gate; `JAN-CSAA-004` SHALL record whether each area requires an Analysis Rule Profile, a Repository Gate Profile, or both:

- composite implementation correctness or readiness for a declared obligation scope, aggregating applicable technical profiles while preserving inconclusive, stale, unsupported, conflicting, and unresolved outcomes;
- syntax and type correctness;
- contract conformance;
- architecture-boundary conformance;
- dependency integrity and cycles;
- dead or unreachable code;
- coupling and change amplification;
- behavioral preservation;
- test adequacy;
- security weakness and taint flow;
- third-party dependency and software-supply-chain exposure;
- unsafe input and output handling;
- concurrency and asynchronous-control risks;
- error and recovery behavior;
- observability sufficiency;
- maintainability and complexity;
- semantic-index freshness and completeness.

Each Analysis Rule Profile or Repository Gate Profile SHALL define:

- stable rule-profile or repository-gate ID and version;
- purpose and protected engineering decision;
- subject and applicability;
- claim evaluated;
- evidence required;
- criteria and epistemic outcomes;
- severity and confidence treatment;
- blocking and non-blocking conditions;
- recognized authority owning the binding enforcement obligation;
- authoritative, non-bypassable enforcement boundary and its versioned enforcement artifact;
- bypass resistance, failure behavior, and the distinction between binding enforcement and local convenience checks;
- any registered interim enforcement carrier when the definitive boundary is not yet available;
- required independence, when any;
- eligible analyzer capabilities;
- false-positive and disagreement handling;
- remediation and reanalysis expectations;
- exception authority and required record;
- evidence-retention requirements.

A Repository Gate Evaluation SHALL be a subject- and revision-bound result referencing the exact Repository Gate Profile, rule-profile versions, inputs, Analyzer Finding Records, exceptions, capability coverage, and execution health used. It SHALL NOT contain a second mutable copy of gate criteria.

A Repository Gate Profile SHALL NOT confer its own authority. Local or pre-commit execution MAY provide early feedback but SHALL NOT be described as the binding guarantee; the profile SHALL cite the recognized authority and non-bypassable enforcement boundary that make a result consequential.

Where a technical gate is mapped into canonical assurance, the document SHALL cite the governing `JPWB-DOC-002`/`JPWB-DOC-003` meaning and SHALL define the mapping without duplicating it.

The analyzer-provider contract in `JAN-CSAA-004` owns the tool-neutral capability obligations. It SHALL require every provider to declare:

- capability IDs implemented;
- supported language and construct versions;
- inputs and configuration;
- outputs and raw-result retention;
- version and rule-set identity;
- completeness and failure signals;
- deterministic/reproducible behavior limits;
- resource and timeout behavior;
- security and licensing constraints;
- normalization and deduplication behavior;
- known semantic loss.

A provider SHALL recommend or report. It SHALL NOT approve its own Analyzer Finding Record, waive a rule or governing Assurance Policy, create a canonical assurance disposition, or mutate source code as an implicit side effect of analysis.

`JAN-CSAA-007` owns the exact adapter shape. `JAN-CSAA-011` selects, versions, configures, secures, and operates concrete providers against this abstract contract; it SHALL NOT redefine capability or evidence meaning.

### 10.5 `JAN-CSAA-005` — Current JPWB repository inventory

This document SHALL be evidence-first and regenerated or revised whenever repository reality changes materially.

It SHALL inventory:

- workspaces, packages, apps, and TypeScript project configurations;
- package DAG and intended dependency constraints;
- source/generated/test/configuration/vendor classifications;
- schema and generated-contract sources;
- build, lint, type, test, coverage, boundary, and static-analysis commands;
- architecture rules and accepted exceptions;
- runtime and test entry points;
- dynamic loading, reflection, code generation, and framework seams;
- existing observability and trace sources;
- existing analyzers and exact checked-in configuration;
- missing semantic capabilities;
- representative success and failure paths;
- current known quality or security Analyzer Finding Records relevant to the tool-suite design;
- confidence for every current-state claim.

The initial inventory SHALL verify, rather than assume, the roles of:

- the TypeScript compiler;
- ESLint;
- dependency-cruiser;
- Vitest;
- `svelte-check` and the Svelte compiler;
- Playwright test, result, and trace evidence, kept distinct from source-coverage evidence;
- checked-in Sonar configuration and any separately verified runner;
- repository build orchestration.

The inventory SHALL explicitly record the checked-in analysis exclusions, app-specific gates, and missing repository-wide coverage configuration. Candidate tools absent from the repository SHALL be labeled candidates, not current capabilities.

### 10.6 `JAN-CSAA-006` — Golden fixture

The fixture strategy SHALL contain both:

1. a small synthetic repository whose declared expected semantic facts and Analyzer Finding Records are independently and exhaustively reviewable within a stated coverage boundary; and
2. selected, version-pinned JPWB scenarios that demonstrate realistic monorepo, generated-contract, architecture-boundary, test, and runtime behavior.

The fixture SHALL include known-positive and known-negative cases for:

- symbol aliasing and re-exports;
- authored `.svelte` source to generated or virtual TypeScript mapping, including diagnostic-location fidelity and deliberately broken mappings;
- generics and overload resolution;
- inheritance and interface implementation;
- dynamic dispatch;
- unreachable branches;
- async and exception control flow;
- data flow from source through sanitizer to sink;
- unsafe source-to-sink flow;
- dependency cycles and forbidden package edges;
- package-manifest and lockfile changes, resolved-version deltas, and vulnerable dependency/advisory correlation, including unknown or conflicting advisory cases;
- dead-code candidates with and without dynamic entry points;
- contract-compatible and incompatible changes;
- coupling increase and decrease;
- test-to-code mapping;
- coverage change;
- runtime paths that confirm or contradict static candidates;
- matched and mismatched build-artifact, source-map, instrumentation, test-selection, coverage, trace-schema, collector, workload, and environment provenance;
- incremental edit, rename, move, configuration change, and deletion;
- stale-cache and interrupted-index recovery.

Expected results SHALL be authored independently of the analyzer under test. A provider's output SHALL NOT be copied into the golden oracle as the expected judgment.

Fixture and oracle governance SHALL inherit the two-stream and authority-separation rules of `JPWB-DOC-004`: implementation authors MAY propose new cases, but an authorized independent reviewer SHALL confer or change judgment-grain expected results; every oracle change SHALL preserve author, reviewer, rationale, source, subject, and version provenance; unreviewed expectations SHALL remain visibly proposed and SHALL NOT produce a green conformance claim; and a suspected-wrong oracle SHALL create an explicit divergence for adjudication rather than being weakened to fit the implementation.

### 10.7 `JAN-CSAA-007` — Machine contract package

This document SHALL define versioned contracts for:

- subject and snapshot identity;
- source artifact descriptors;
- authored/generated/virtual artifact provenance and source-origin mappings;
- build artifacts, instrumentation or collection configurations, and execution identities;
- semantic nodes and edges;
- provenance and epistemic status;
- analysis requests and runs;
- provider capabilities and health;
- query requests, responses, and explanations;
- change sets and graph deltas;
- test selections, runs, results, and assertion outcomes;
- coverage observations, regions, denominator bases, and granularities;
- runtime-trace observations, schemas, collectors, workload selections, and runtime-artifact mappings;
- Analyzer Finding Records and Engineering Evidence Records;
- Analysis Rule Profile results, Repository Gate Evaluations, and governed assurance mappings;
- exceptions and suppressions;
- invalidation and freshness;
- authorization context, repository/worktree scope, and audit references;
- diagnostics and typed errors;
- agent-facing tool calls or API operations.

The package SHALL define:

- designated schema source files;
- generated TypeScript types;
- schema and contract versions;
- compatibility and migration rules;
- unknown-field behavior;
- pagination and bounded-result behavior;
- deterministic ordering where required;
- partial-result semantics;
- cancellation, timeout, and resource-exhaustion errors;
- safe redaction;
- provider raw-result references;
- validation stages.

Machine contracts SHALL be implemented as enforced repository reference artifacts. Prose examples SHALL NOT become a competing shape authority.

### 10.8 `JAN-CSAA-008` — Executable conformance and V&V

This document SHALL convert the semantic and assurance obligations into an executable acceptance contract.

The test catalog SHALL include:

- schema and generated-type conformance;
- semantic-model invariant tests;
- golden AST, symbol, type, dependency, call, CFG, DFG, and CPG tests;
- authored-framework-to-virtual-source provenance and diagnostic-location fidelity tests;
- resolved dependency, lockfile-delta, advisory-correlation, and vulnerability-observation tests;
- cross-graph identity and provenance tests;
- provider-adapter contract tests;
- differential tests between overlapping providers;
- property and metamorphic tests;
- mutation tests proving rules fail when their target defect is introduced;
- incremental invalidation and cache-correctness tests;
- stale, incomplete, unsupported, conflicting, timeout, and partial-result tests;
- security tests for untrusted repositories and analyzer execution;
- deterministic replay and reproducibility tests;
- performance and resource-budget tests;
- coverage and trace correlation tests;
- negative build/instrumentation/test/coverage/trace identity and observation-cutoff tests;
- coding-agent trajectory tests for mandatory analysis use;
- negative tests proving no false green state is emitted.

Every test SHALL have:

- a stable ID;
- governing requirement references;
- fixture and subject version;
- preconditions;
- action;
- expected semantic result;
- expected evidence and diagnostics;
- failure meaning.

The suite SHALL test the tests. Critical rules SHALL have mutation or kill tests demonstrating that a vacuous implementation cannot pass.

### 10.9 `JAN-CSAA-009` — Persistence, incrementality, recovery, and operations

This document SHALL define:

- immutable or content-addressed snapshot identity;
- atomic publication of a complete index revision;
- incremental invalidation and recomputation;
- dependency-aware cache invalidation;
- concurrent reads and writes;
- worktree and multi-branch isolation;
- interrupted analysis and restart;
- provider failure and degraded capability;
- reindex and rebuild;
- storage schema evolution;
- retention, redaction, and source confidentiality;
- query authorization and least-privilege access;
- repository, worktree, branch, and tenant isolation where applicable;
- encryption requirements for persisted and transported sensitive data;
- integrity, corruption detection, and repair;
- security-relevant audit events;
- garbage collection;
- backup and recovery;
- telemetry, health, readiness, and capacity;
- dual-run comparison during engine or provider migration;
- fallback and cutover;
- prevention of mixed-revision query results.

A query SHALL resolve against one declared semantic snapshot unless it explicitly requests and labels a cross-snapshot comparison.

An incremental result SHALL be observationally equivalent, within documented ordering rules and inference limits, to a clean full analysis of the same subject.

Static semantic-snapshot identity SHALL remain distinct from execution-evidence-set identity. A query combining static and execution evidence SHALL declare both identities and MAY additionally declare an observation cutoff. Newly arriving test results, coverage, or traces SHALL create an append-only evidence-set revision and SHALL NOT mutate the historical result of a prior query.

### 10.10 `JAN-CSAA-010` — Coding-agent analysis employment profile

This document SHALL specialize `JPWB-DOC-004`; it SHALL NOT restate the general Agent Operating Protocol.

It SHALL map change risk and employment points to the Analysis Rule Profiles and Repository Gate Profiles owned by `JAN-CSAA-004`; it SHALL NOT redefine their evidence criteria, outcomes, severity, or blocking semantics.

It SHALL define the analyses, evidence, stop conditions, and reporting obligations for:

| Employment point | Minimum CSAA purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Repository intake | Establish subject, projects, packages, generated boundaries, architecture rules, tool availability, and index freshness |
| Before design | Locate existing implementations, contracts, callers, dependencies, tests, runtime paths, and constraints |
| Before planning | Predict affected semantic closure, risk, required tests, gates, migrations, and reanalysis |
| During implementation | Refresh affected facts; surface new type, dependency, control-flow, data-flow, and security Analyzer Finding Records early |
| After a coherent increment | Compare semantic delta with planned impact and rerun affected Analysis Rule Profiles and Repository Gate Evaluations |
| Local pre-commit checkpoint | Run the change-scope checks required for early feedback; reject stale, skipped, or falsely green evidence, but do not describe this bypassable client-side check as the binding guarantee |
| Before merge | Rebind the subject to the actual target-branch merge state, recompute cross-branch impact, and run the applicable integration and regression gates |
| Regression analysis | Compare the declared before/after subjects, tests, coverage, traces, Analyzer Finding Records, and behavior or contract evidence without substituting line diffs for semantic change |
| Before completion | Run the full applicable evidence ladder, close the requirement ledger, and account for every Analyzer Finding Record |
| Refactoring | Prove intended API, dependency, call, and behavior preservation at the declared boundary |
| Defect investigation | Join static paths, tests, coverage, traces, failures, and change history without patching by guess |
| Security work | Run applicable pattern, data-flow, dependency, boundary, and adversarial analyses |
| Handoff | Report subject revision, analyses run, coverage limits, Analyzer Finding Records, exceptions, residual risk, and unverified areas |

The profile SHALL define:

- mandatory versus discretionary analyses by change risk and affected concern;
- cheapest-relevant-proof ordering;
- how an agent queries and cites semantic evidence;
- how Analyzer Finding Records change a plan;
- when reanalysis is required;
- how conflicting analyzer evidence is reconciled or escalated;
- when an incomplete or stale index blocks work;
- how approved exceptions are represented;
- what evidence is sufficient for a completion claim;
- what must be reported when a capability is unavailable.

Human inspection, explanation, comparison, and adjudication interfaces SHALL remain allocated to `JAN-CSAA-001`, `JAN-CSAA-003`, and `JAN-CSAA-007`. `JAN-CSAA-010` SHALL govern only coding-agent employment of those capabilities.

The agent SHALL NOT:

- treat a semantic index as fresher than its subject binding proves;
- cite a graph edge without provenance;
- silently dismiss an analyzer disagreement;
- suppress an Analyzer Finding Record merely to make a gate green;
- claim dead code solely from missing static callers when dynamic entry is unresolved;
- claim behavioral preservation solely from type checking or unchanged line coverage;
- claim security solely from one tool's absence of Analyzer Finding Records;
- claim a required check passed when it did not run.

### 10.11 `JAN-CSAA-011` — TypeScript and tool integration

This document SHALL be authored only after a separately authorized Wave 4 commission identifies the exact `JAN-CSAA-002`, `JAN-CSAA-004`, `JAN-CSAA-006`, `JAN-CSAA-007`, and `JAN-CSAA-008` versions that form its qualification baseline and records any permissible unresolved constraints. Without that recorded gate, `JAN-CSAA-011` authoring and provider selection remain blocked.

Provider selection SHALL NOT proceed without that recorded gate.

It SHALL assign a bounded role to each selected provider and SHALL document rejected overlap.

The first qualification set MAY evaluate the candidates below, but Stage A selects or approves none of them:

- TypeScript Compiler API and `ts-morph` for parsing, program structure, symbol resolution, type semantics, ergonomics, and provenance-preserving abstraction;
- `svelte-check` and Svelte compiler integration for authored `.svelte` TypeScript, generated/virtual-code provenance, and source-location fidelity;
- dependency-cruiser, ESLint, TypeScript ESLint, and checked-in Sonar configuration as current repository surfaces whose exact capability and evidentiary roles require qualification;
- Vitest, coverage candidates, and instrumentation configurations for revision-bound test and coverage observations;
- Playwright for end-to-end observations and candidate trace evidence, kept distinct from source coverage;
- Semgrep, Tree-sitter, Joern, and CodeQL only as unselected candidates subject to semantic fit, loss, disagreement, licensing, redistribution, privacy, installation, and execution-model review;
- graph stores or libraries only after query, portability, operations, and cost evaluation.

The standard SHALL decide:

- designated primary extraction or normalization, corroborating, and fallback provider roles per capability and derived fact type, each with bounded coverage, provenance, disagreement retention, and no independent semantic or governance authority;
- provider overlap and conflict precedence;
- deduplication without evidence loss;
- version pinning and reproducibility;
- local, CI, and isolated execution profiles;
- licensing and redistribution;
- sandboxing and network access;
- source-code confidentiality;
- installation and update rules;
- performance budgets;
- provider health and degraded behavior;
- qualification and removal criteria.

No provider SHALL be selected because it was named in the background chat. Selection SHALL be supported by a qualification record against `JAN-CSAA-006` and `JAN-CSAA-008`.

---

## 11. Assurance-question closure matrix

The corpus SHALL preserve the assurance-question evidence, corroboration, rule-or-claim owner, and executable-or-corroborating-document mappings below:

| Assurance question | Primary semantic evidence | Required corroboration or boundary | Rule or claim owner | Executable or corroborating documents |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Is the implementation correct for the declared obligation scope? | Applicable governed requirements/contracts, compiler evidence, semantic analyses, tests/results, coverage, runtime observations, and unresolved Analyzer Finding Records | Correctness is a composite outcome and MAY remain inconclusive; no analyzer-conformance fixture proves an arbitrary subject correct | Governing obligation owners; 004 owns only a technical aggregation profile | 003, 006, 008, 010 |
| Is the implementation syntactically and type-correct? | AST, compiler diagnostics, symbols, types | Supported project/config coverage and successful build/type gate | 004 | 002, 003, 008 |
| Does it violate architectural constraints? | Package/module dependency graph and declared rules | Concern-owning architecture authority, exceptions, and dynamic/runtime dependency limits | Recognized concern-owning authority, including any ADR only through its recognized carrier; 004 maps it into a technical profile | 003, 005, 008 |
| Is there dead code? | Symbols, references, call graph, CFG, entry-point model | Reflection/framework/generated-code model, tests, coverage, and traces; otherwise report candidate | 004 | 003, 006, 008 |
| Is a contract violated? | Types, schemas, imports/calls, boundary data flow | Enforced reference artifact, governed compatibility rule, and contract tests | Governing contract owner; 004 operationalizes the technical profile | 002, 007, 008 |
| Does the change increase coupling? | Before/after dependency and call graphs | Declared metric, architecture meaning, acceptable exception, and affected closure | 004 | 003, 005, 008 |
| Does it introduce a security vulnerability? | Pattern, dependency, control-flow, data-flow, taint, and boundary evidence | Threat model, capability coverage, exploitability review, adversarial tests, and uncertainty | 004 | 003, 006, 008 |
| Does it preserve intended behavior? | Semantic delta, tests, coverage, runtime traces | Governed requirement/contract, behavior-level oracle, relevant negative paths, and accepted residual limits | Governing behavior/contract owner; 004 operationalizes the technical profile | 003, 006, 008, 010 |
| Is the analysis itself trustworthy? | Provider qualification, provenance, golden/differential/mutation results | Fresh subject binding, coverage declaration, health, and no unresolved critical conformance failure | 008 | 004, 006, 009, 011 |

No single row SHALL equate one evidence source with a complete proof.

Each technical Analysis Rule Profile and Repository Gate Profile, and any governed assurance mapping that consumes it, SHALL define applicable supported, violated, inconclusive, stale, and unsupported outcomes.

---

## 12. Horizontal closure matrix

Each concern SHALL have one semantic owner and at least one executable verification target.

| Concern | Semantic owner | Shape owner | Verification owner | Operational owner |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Subject and revision identity | 002 | 007 | 008 | 009 |
| Code object and relation meanings, including AST, symbols, types, dependencies, calls, CFG, DFG, taint, and CPG | 002 | 007 | 006 and 008 | 011 |
| Test, coverage, runtime, and trace observation meanings | 002 | 007 | 006 and 008 | 009 |
| Extraction, enrichment, inference, query, slicing, correlation, and semantic-delta meanings | 003 | 007 | 006 and 008 | 009 and 011 |
| Analysis Rule Profiles, Analyzer Finding Records, Repository Gate Profiles, and Repository Gate Evaluations | 004 | 007 | 008 | 010 and 011 |
| Intended JPWB architecture obligations | Recognized concern-owning authority, including an ADR only through its canon, ratified-specification, or registered-working-reference carrier | Enforced repository configurations and rules | Existing gates plus 008 | 010 and 011 |
| Current JPWB implementation inventory and conformance mapping | 005, descriptively only | 005 plus machine inventory contracts in 007 | Repository/configuration inspection and 008 | 005 refresh procedure |
| Fixture scenarios and expected-result records | 006 | Fixture artifacts | 008 plus independent oracle review | 006 change procedure |
| Machine serialization and adapter compatibility | 007 | Enforced schemas and generated types | 008 | 009 and 011 |
| Analyzer conformance and V&V method | 008 | Test catalog and executable tests | Independent review and mutation evidence | CI and qualification runs |
| Freshness-state meaning | 002 | 007 | 008 | 009 |
| Invalidation, recomputation, persistence, and recovery behavior | 009 | Storage and operations contracts | 008 | 009 |
| General coding-agent procedure | `JPWB-DOC-004` | Operative protocol | Operative protocol verification | `JPWB-DOC-004` |
| CSAA employment-point and gate-profile invocation mapping | 010 | Agent-facing contract in 007 | Trajectory tests in 008 | 010 |
| Abstract analyzer-provider capability obligations and conflict semantics | 004 | Adapter contracts in 007 | 006 and 008 | 011 |
| Concrete provider selection, version, configuration, licensing, and execution profile | 011 | Integration configuration | Qualification suite in 006 and 008 | 011 |

A specialization SHALL cite its base semantic owner and SHALL NOT become a co-owner by restating the meaning. If two documents define incompatible meaning for the same concern, that is a corpus defect. It SHALL fail review or conformance rather than be silently reconciled in implementation.

